294	# Architecture
295	
296	Mekiki is a command line agent that recommends npm packages. You ask a question, it looks up real registry data through tools, and it answers with one pick and up to two alternatives.
297	
298	## Files
299	
300	| File              | Purpose                                                                  |
301	| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
302	| `agent.ts`        | The whole agent: tool schemas, tool functions, and the loop              |
303	| `README.md`       | What Mekiki is, setup, and things to try                                 |
304	| `.env`            | `OPENAI_API_KEY`, `OPENAI_MODEL` and `NEATLOGS_API_KEY`, never committed |
305	| `architecture.md` | How the agent works                                                      |
306	| `decisions.md`    | Why it works that way, and what we rejected                              |
307	| `evals.md`        | Test questions, their answer keys, and scored results                    |
308	
309	## The loop
310	
311	`runAgent` keeps a `messages` array and calls the model with it and the tool list. If the reply asks for tools, the loop runs them in parallel with `Promise.all`, appends each result tagged with its `tool_call_id`, and calls the model again. If the reply has no tool calls, that text is the answer. `MAX_STEPS` caps the number of model turns, not tool calls, since one turn can request several tools in parallel.
312	
313	```mermaid
314	flowchart LR
315	  Q[Question] --> M[Model call]
316	  M -->|tool_calls| T[Run tools]
317	  T -->|results by tool_call_id| M
318	  M -->|no tool_calls| A[Answer]
319	```
320	
321	## Tools
322	
323	Each tool has two halves. The schema in `tools` is what the model reads. The function is plain TypeScript that the loop runs on the model's behalf.
324	
325	| Tool                     | Source                                                  | Returns                                                                                                                            |
326	| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
327	| `search_packages(query)` | npm search API, 25 results                              | Top 8 by weekly downloads: name, description, downloads                                                                            |
328	| `get_package_info(name)` | `/<name>/latest` plus an exact-name search, in parallel | Description, version, last publish date, months since that publish, license, repository, downloads, deprecation, or `found: false` |
329	
330	Tool results go back as JSON strings and stay in the context for every later turn, so each tool returns only the fields the model needs.
331	
332	## How it picks
333	
334	The system prompt is a numbered process. The model first names the packages it knows developers choose for the job, then checks them with `get_package_info` while `search_packages` looks for anything it missed. Its knowledge says what people choose. The tools say whether those packages exist, are maintained, and how widely they're installed. Packages that share a `repository` count as one project.
335	
336	## Tracing
337	
338	Neatlogs records every run as one trace:
339	
340	```
341	WORKFLOW advise
342	└─ AGENT npm_advisor
343	   ├─ LLM (one per model turn, from wrapOpenAI)
344	   ├─ TOOL search_packages
345	   └─ TOOL get_package_info
346	```
347	
348	`init()` runs once at startup. `openai` is the only OpenAI client, wrapped with `wrapOpenAI`, so no model call escapes the trace. Tool functions are wrapped with `span({ kind: 'TOOL' })`, which records their inputs and outputs. `shutdown()` runs in a `finally` block so spans get sent even when the run crashes.
349	
350	## Known gaps
351	
352	- If npm returns an error status, the tool throws and the whole run crashes. The model never gets a chance to recover.
353	- Deprecated packages often drop out of npm search, so their publish date and downloads come back `null`.
354	- `get_package_info` confirms a package exists and is maintained, not that it fits the job. If the model names the wrong package from memory, nothing catches it.
355	- Search results depend heavily on wording. The tool description asks the model to try several phrasings.
356	# Decisions
357	
358	Newest first. Each entry says what we chose, why, and what we turned down.
359	
360	## 2026-10-08: Compute package age in code
361	 
362	**Problem.** The holdout set scored 5 of 9 on main picks and walked into a trap 2 of 9 times. Every miss involved a package last published in 2023. In traces `55ecba05902f932bf329ac6d8ffd07d9` and `1b71c2d78fead71c8246242d2a2f624f` the model called `node-fetch` "actively maintained" with "Last Published: July 25, 2023" printed right above.
363	 
364	**Root cause.** The model doesn't know today's date. gpt-4o-mini's training data ends around late 2023, so a 2023 date looks recent to it. The prompt said "weigh last publish date," but that means comparing to today, which the model can't do.
365	 
366	**Fix.** `get_package_info` now returns `monthsSinceLastPublish`, computed in TypeScript. The prompt replaces "weigh" with a rule: at 12 months or more, never the main pick, and any alternative must state its age. It also says high downloads don't mean a package is maintained, since `node-fetch` has 249M weekly downloads.
367	 
368	Rejected: putting today's date in the system prompt. The model would still have to subtract dates, and small models do arithmetic badly. Code is exact. We kept this to one mechanism so the result is easy to attribute.
369	 
370	**Tradeoff.** Some packages are finished and rarely need releases. A 12 month rule will flag them. For "which library should I pick" questions, an old release is a fair warning, and the rule only blocks the main pick, so such packages can still show up as alternatives with their age stated.
371	 
372	**Result.** Mixed. On the holdout set, main picks went from 5 of 9 to 9 of 9, but alternatives fell from 14 of 18 to 8 of 18, and traps went from 2 of 9 to 3 of 9. Full scores and which predictions held are in `evals.md`.
373	 
374	The traces show three ways the alternatives broke:
375	 
376	1. The hard line at 12 months threw out `luxon` at 13 months in every H1 run, instead of keeping it with a warning.
377	2. With freshness as the loudest signal, fresh and popular packages took slots whether or not they fit: `moment` (released last month, but legacy by its own team's account) in H1, `gaxios` (a Google APIs client) in H2.
378	3. The model's own candidate list is stale. Once its 2023-era picks were ruled out, it had too few good ones left and filled slots from search, with packages like `constate` and `unstated-next`. It reads "at most two alternatives" as "exactly two."
379	So the fix traded one failure for others. We keep the computed age, since it's right that the model can't do date math, and change how the prompt uses it.
380	
381	## 2026-10-07: The model proposes, the tools verify
382	
383	**Problem.** On "best React form library?", the main pick was right in 5 of 5 runs, but the alternatives were wrong in 10 of 10. They were `rc-field-form`, `@rc-component/form` and `survey-react-ui`. The first two are the same library under an old and a new name, and both ship inside Ant Design, so their downloads come from antd installs, not developers choosing them. `survey-react-ui` got picked because its description says "React form library," word for word. Formik and TanStack Form never appeared.
384	
385	**Root cause, from the traces.** Downloads measure installs, not choices, so anything bundled inside a popular library looks popular. And the model only checked what search handed it. In trace `ad6ff271963c7520c05d8bf6c328352a`, search returned `@tanstack/react-form-devtools`, direct evidence TanStack Form exists, and the model never looked up `@tanstack/react-form`. The prompt line "plus any well-known package search missed" had no effect.
386	
387	 
388	**Fix.** Two changes aimed at one metric:
389	
390	1. The system prompt is now numbered steps. Step 1 has the model name the packages developers usually choose, from its own knowledge, and look them up in its first turn alongside search. The model knows what people choose but its knowledge is stale. The tools are current but can't tell a choice from a dependency. Each covers the other's gap: a made-up or abandoned name comes back as `found: false` or with an old publish date.
391	2. `get_package_info` now returns `repository`, normalized so `git+https://...git` and `github:owner/repo` compare equal. Two names with the same repository count as one project. In a monorepo this groups related packages too (`@tanstack/react-form` and its devtools share `github.com/tanstack/form`), which is what we want for alternatives. We changed both together since they target the same failure. If the score improves, a follow-up run without the repository field would show how much each one did.
392	
393	 
394	**Metric.** Per run, how many of the two alternatives are `formik`, `@tanstack/react-form` or `react-final-form`. Scored 0, 1 or 2. Defined before seeing any results.
395	
396	 
397	| | Runs | Alternatives score | Main pick right |
398	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
399	| Before | 5 | 0 of 10 | 5 of 5 |
400	| After | 5 | 10 of 10 | 5 of 5 |
401	
402	 
403	After, the alternatives were Formik plus TanStack Form once, and Formik plus React Final Form four times. The rc packages and `survey-react-ui` never appeared as picks. Trace `f32a5943ac347bdd4e6af422a0dfa485` shows the new behavior: in its first turn the model looked up `formik`, `react-hook-form`, `redux-form` and `@tanstack/react-form` from its own knowledge, alongside one search. It dropped `redux-form` after seeing it was last published in March 2023, so the verify half works too.
404	
405	 
406	Average run time also fell from 11.7 s to 9.6 s, since the model often reached an answer in two model turns instead of three. We didn't aim for this, and the runs did different amounts of work, so treat it as a side effect, not a measured win.
407	
408	 
409	**What's still wrong.**
410	
411	- In 2 of 5 runs the model "remembered" `@tanstack/react-query`, a data fetching library, as a form library. It exists, so `get_package_info` returned `found: true`. Our verification checks that a package exists and is maintained, not that it fits the job. The model didn't recommend it, but nothing stopped it.
412	- In trace `bcf477fbb559700244846c9afb665a15` the answer listed `@hookform/resolvers` and `survey-react-ui` as "additional packages," outside the one pick and two alternatives the prompt allows.
413	- In trace `bdbbaf8c1fe6b585e118c48660ee173c` the model searched in a separate turn instead of the first one, which cost a fourth model call.
414	- In the 4 runs that picked React Final Form (647K weekly downloads), TanStack Form (4.2M, published more recently) lost out. In 2 of those, the model never looked TanStack Form up because it checked `@tanstack/react-query` instead.
415	- All 10 runs used one question. The fix may be tuned to it.
416	
417	## 2026-10-07: Run tool calls in parallel
418	
419	The model often asks for several tools in one turn, but the loop ran them one at a time with `await` inside `for...of`. The answer was the same either way, so only the trace timeline showed it: each tool span started the moment the previous one ended. Now the loop runs them with `Promise.all`. Each result comes back paired with its `tool_call_id`, so order can't get mixed up.
420	
421	Measured on the form question, comparing runs with the same number of calls (3 searches, then 3 package lookups):
422	
423	|               | Before | After  |
424	| ------------- | ------ | ------ |
425	| Time in tools | 6.3 s  | 2.3 s  |
426	| Total run     | 14.0 s | 10.7 s |
427	
428	Before: trace `ad6ff271963c7520c05d8bf6c328352a`. After: trace `06f94df4908c636d9e010c1031cbc07e`. Two more runs after the fix made only 2 searches and spent 2.8 s and 2.9 s in tools. Answer quality didn't change, which is what we expected from a speed-only fix.
429	
430	The model calls now take most of the time. The final call alone runs 4 to 5 seconds, since it writes 250 to 300 tokens.
431	
432	## 2026-10-07: Manual Neatlogs setup, not the wizard
433	
434	Neatlogs offers `npx @neatlogs/wizard@latest`, which edits your code for you. We added tracing by hand so we know what every line does before the hackathon, where we'll have to shape traces ourselves.
435	
436	The trace tree is `WORKFLOW advise` > `AGENT npm_advisor` > `LLM` spans from `wrapOpenAI` and `TOOL` spans from `span()`. One CLI run is one trace.
437	
438	The API key comes from `NEATLOGS_API_KEY` in `.env`. The dashboard snippet hardcodes it in `init()`, which would put it in a public repo.
439	
440	`shutdown()` runs in a `finally` block. Spans flush every 5 seconds, so a short run or a crash could exit before they're sent. The `finally` matters most for crashes, since those are the traces we most want to see.
441	
442	## 2026-10-07: One `get_package_info` tool instead of two
443	
444	We merged the planned `get_package_info` and `get_downloads` into one tool. The model never wants one of those facts without the others, and every extra tool is one more choice it can get wrong. The cost is two HTTP requests inside one tool call.
445	
446	## 2026-10-07: `/latest` plus search, not the full package document
447	
448	The full document for `react-hook-form` is 5.2 MB because it lists every version ever published. `/latest` is 5 KB and has version, description, license and deprecation, but no publish date. An exact-name search fills in publish date and weekly downloads. Both run in parallel.
449	
450	Rejected: fetching the latest version first and then its description. `/latest` returns both in one request, so a second call adds nothing.
451	
452	Rejected: the abbreviated document (`application/vnd.npm.install-v1+json`). Still 2.6 MB, and it only has a `modified` date, which also changes on edits that aren't releases.
453	
454	## 2026-10-07: A missing package is data, not an error
455	
456	`get_package_info` returns `{ found: false }` on a 404. A model guessing a package name that doesn't exist is an expected outcome, and it should learn that and move on. A 500 or a network failure is different, and it still throws.
457	
458	## 2026-10-07: Leave the crash on npm errors in, for now
459	
460	A failed npm request crashes the run instead of returning the error to the model. We left it on purpose so we can trigger it and find it in Neatlogs traces in step 4.
461	
462	## 2026-10-07: Fetch 25 search results, sort by downloads, keep 8
463	
464	npm search ranks by text match, not popularity. With 5 results, "React form library" missed `react-hook-form` (70M weekly downloads) entirely. With 25 it came first after sorting. Keeping 8 limits how much text rides along in the context on every later turn.
465	
466	## 2026-10-07: Phrasing advice lives in the tool description
467	
468	"react form" and "react form library" return very different results, so the tool description tells the model to try 2 or 3 phrasings. We put this in the tool description rather than the system prompt because it's about using this one tool, and it travels with the tool if we reuse it.
469	
470	## 2026-10-07: Model name comes from `OPENAI_MODEL`
471	
472	Model names change often. An env var lets us swap models without touching code. We use `gpt-4o-mini`, a cheap model that fails often enough to teach us something.
473	
474	## 2026-10-07: `tsx` and Node's `--env-file`
475	
476	`tsx` runs TypeScript with no build step. Node 22's `--env-file` loads `.env`, so we skipped `dotenv`. `openai` 7.x needs Node 22 or later anyway.
477	# Evals
478	
479	Each question has its answer key written down before any run. Commit this file before running, so the git history shows the key came first and nobody moved the goalposts after seeing results.
480	
481	## How to score a run
482	
483	| Field | Meaning                                                                                           |
484	| ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
485	| Main  | Is the main pick in the **accept** list? Yes or no.                                               |
486	| Alts  | How many of the alternatives are in the **accept** list, 0 to 2.                                  |
487	| Trap  | Did the answer recommend anything from the **trap** list, as main pick or alternative? Yes or no. |
488	
489	Packages in neither list score nothing and aren't a trap. Something mentioned outside the pick and alternatives, like an "also found" list, doesn't count toward Main or Alts, but write it down as a note.
